Just as in classification, precision and recall trade off against each other in retrieval: retrieving more results (larger k) tends to improve recall (you're more likely to have caught the relevant ones somewhere in there) but can hurt precision (more irrelevant results mixed in) — the exact same tension from the ML course's metrics lesson, now applied to a ranked list instead of a binary classification decision.

```python
# Conceptual: recall@k and precision@k
def recall_at_k(retrieved, relevant, k):
    top_k = retrieved[:k]
    return len(set(top_k) & set(relevant)) / len(relevant)

def precision_at_k(retrieved, relevant, k):
    top_k = retrieved[:k]
    return len(set(top_k) & set(relevant)) / k
```
